WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.845 --> 00:00:03.770
A piece of grid, from substation to factory.

2
00:00:04.765 --> 00:00:09.176
A load flow computes, for all demand and generation at one moment,

3
00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:13.949
the current through every cable and transformer and the voltage at every point.

4
00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:19.720
Per asset two numbers: the loading, current as a percentage of what it may carry,

5
00:00:19.905 --> 00:00:22.227
and the voltage as a percentage of nominal.

6
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.153
Red is above 100 percent or outside the band.

7
00:00:27.585 --> 00:00:32.438
Rating: the current a cable or transformer carries continuously without overheating.

8
00:00:33.153 --> 00:00:35.533
For a cable it depends on cross-section,

9
00:00:35.637 --> 00:00:38.876
soil type, soil temperature and nearby cables.

10
00:00:39.911 --> 00:00:40.584
Rule of thumb:

11
00:00:40.735 --> 00:00:46.679
a common aluminium medium-voltage cable carries roughly 250 to 300 amps;

12
00:00:46.819 --> 00:00:48.096
at 10 kilovolts,

13
00:00:48.235 --> 00:00:52.020
4 to 5 megavolt amperes, about as many megawatts.

14
00:00:53.012 --> 00:00:56.599
Current is power divided by root three times the voltage:

15
00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:02.845
2.5 megavolt amperes at 10 kilovolts is about 144 amps.

16
00:01:04.188 --> 00:01:09.529
This cable is a feeder: one outgoing route from the substation, with stations on it.

17
00:01:10.527 --> 00:01:14.695
The station transformer carries the sum of feeders that don’t peak together:

18
00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:19.025
one with a business park or three solar farms at 100 percent,

19
00:01:19.211 --> 00:01:20.465
the rest at 40.

20
00:01:21.436 --> 00:01:25.395
So green on the station’s capacity map is not room at your address:

21
00:01:25.499 --> 00:01:28.506
check the feeder and the voltage at the end too.

22
00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:33.448
Current through the cable’s resistance costs voltage: a long,

23
00:01:33.645 --> 00:01:36.780
heavily loaded feeder ends low on a winter evening.

24
00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:40.160
With feed-in, putting power onto the grid,

25
00:01:40.323 --> 00:01:43.051
a solar street rises on a sunny afternoon.

26
00:01:44.092 --> 00:01:46.809
At the customer the ten-minute average must nearly

27
00:01:46.878 --> 00:01:51.128
always stay within 230 volts plus or minus 10 percent,

28
00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:57.257
a European standard: 207 to 253 volts, the voltage band.

29
00:01:58.306 --> 00:02:00.628
Too high, and inverters switch off.

30
00:02:01.313 --> 00:02:04.901
The planner splits the band between medium and low voltage.

31
00:02:05.894 --> 00:02:10.201
Two worst moments: offtake peaks on a winter evening, with heat pumps,

32
00:02:10.352 --> 00:02:15.077
chargers and little sun; feed-in on a sunny spring afternoon with low demand.

33
00:02:16.145 --> 00:02:20.383
Offtake or feed-in congestion: always ask which one is decisive here.

34
00:02:21.664 --> 00:02:28.363
An example: a 10 kilovolt feeder rated 300 amps peaks at 210 on a winter evening;

35
00:02:28.503 --> 00:02:33.042
its substation has 80 megavolt amperes and peaks at 45.

36
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:40.006
A food factory at the end wants 2.5 megavolt amperes more, mostly evenings.

37
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.181
Sales: the station is barely half full.

38
00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:53.153
That is 144 amps: 210 plus 144 is 354, 118 percent.

39
00:02:53.888 --> 00:02:56.106
The feeder limits, not the station.

40
00:02:56.924 --> 00:02:59.118
Then check the voltage at the end.

41
00:03:00.165 --> 00:03:04.636
Options: move the normally open point, the open switch to the next feeder,

42
00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:08.420
so it takes some load; a dedicated cable from the station; or,

43
00:03:08.571 --> 00:03:13.168
until the reinforcement, non-firm, which can be curtailed, or off-peak.

44
00:03:14.503 --> 00:03:19.159
N minus one: the grid keeps supplying when any single component fails.

45
00:03:19.865 --> 00:03:23.987
N is everything in service, minus one the worst single outage.

46
00:03:25.022 --> 00:03:26.589
A transformer, a cable,

47
00:03:26.729 --> 00:03:28.134
a line, or the busbar:

48
00:03:28.296 --> 00:03:32.128
the thick conductor all incoming and outgoing circuits connect to.

49
00:03:33.143 --> 00:03:37.229
It does not cover two faults at once, or a fault during maintenance,

50
00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.712
when there is often no reserve: so maintain at low load.

51
00:03:41.756 --> 00:03:48.687
Since 1 January 2026 the Energy Act requires it from 110 kilovolts up,

52
00:03:48.804 --> 00:03:52.763
with the transformers to the regional grid: a short break at most.

53
00:03:53.589 --> 00:03:59.592
The old Grid Code allowed up to 10 minutes and 100 megawatts, sometimes 6 hours.

54
00:04:00.614 --> 00:04:03.621
A medium-voltage ring’s reserve is switching over,

55
00:04:03.738 --> 00:04:06.942
within hours; in low voltage you wait for the repair.

56
00:04:08.257 --> 00:04:12.994
Two 40 megavolt ampere transformers give a firm capacity of about 40,

57
00:04:13.191 --> 00:04:16.453
not 80: what is left with the largest component out.

58
00:04:17.536 --> 00:04:20.265
Most of the year half sits idle as reserve;

59
00:04:20.381 --> 00:04:24.421
a pair of cables likewise: half empty and full are both true.

60
00:04:25.419 --> 00:04:29.250
A transformer can run over its rating for a few cold hours,

61
00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:32.363
so planners sometimes count a bit above 40.

62
00:04:33.648 --> 00:04:38.722
With congestion everywhere, operators sell that reserve non-firm: usable normally,

63
00:04:38.884 --> 00:04:42.274
curtailed or disconnected at a fault or in maintenance.

64
00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:45.872
TenneT called this using the emergency lane:

65
00:04:46.011 --> 00:04:48.856
trading security of supply for capacity now.

66
00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:52.028
The contract says who carries that risk;

67
00:04:52.237 --> 00:04:55.012
the control room must curtail within minutes.

68
00:04:55.714 --> 00:04:57.757
Pitfall: sold, but never tested.

69
00:04:58.838 --> 00:05:02.356
Experts argue whether strict N minus one is affordable,

70
00:05:02.484 --> 00:05:05.863
or whether customers prefer a rare break to years of waiting.

71
00:05:06.535 --> 00:05:08.869
Ask: a thermal limit, or the reserve?

72
00:05:09.603 --> 00:05:12.238
And if the other transformer is in maintenance?

73
00:05:13.529 --> 00:05:19.508
An example: two 40 megavolt ampere transformers, peak 46, growing 2 a year,

74
00:05:19.717 --> 00:05:21.575
a new station in 6 years.

75
00:05:22.331 --> 00:05:25.593
Waiting: a cold store of 3 and a battery of 4.

76
00:05:26.609 --> 00:05:32.959
Already 6 over N minus one: if a transformer fails at peak, the other carries 46,

77
00:05:33.064 --> 00:05:34.922
115 percent.

78
00:05:35.698 --> 00:05:40.376
That lasts a few cold hours, if you can switch load to neighbouring stations.

79
00:05:41.406 --> 00:05:42.196
Firm: no.

80
00:05:42.898 --> 00:05:48.680
But 80 minus 46 is 34, idle in normal operation: offer both non-firm,

81
00:05:48.819 --> 00:05:50.990
curtailed when a transformer is out.

82
00:05:52.054 --> 00:05:55.479
And watch the growth: in 3 years the peak is 52.
